{
  "about": "Canonical cross-chain bridge contracts for the chains config/chains.js knows about, seeded as builtin 'bridge' labels by migration 044. Every address was read byte-for-byte out of raw source fetched from the protocol's own site (never a summarizer, never an aggregator, never model memory) and cross-checked against its public block-explorer name tag where the explorer would answer. See backend/scripts/generate-bridge-seed.js.",
  "researchedOn": "2026-07-29",
  "rowDefaults": {
    "user_id": null,
    "source": "builtin-bridge",
    "kind": "bridge",
    "confidence": "high"
  },
  "sources": {
    "arbitrum": "https://docs.arbitrum.io/arbitrum-essentials/reference/contract-addresses",
    "linea": "https://docs.linea.build/network/build/contracts",
    "optimism": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ethereum-optimism/superchain-registry/main/superchain/extra/addresses/addresses.json",
    "across": "https://docs.across.to/chains-and-contracts",
    "polygon": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/maticnetwork/static/master/network/mainnet/v1/index.json",
    "gnosis": "https://docs.gnosischain.com/bridges/About%20Token%20Bridges/xdai-bridge",
    "zksync": "https://docs.zksync.io/zksync-protocol/contracts/l1-contracts/zk-chain-addresses",
    "zksync-lite": "https://docs.lite.zksync.io/api/environments/"
  },
  "sourceNotes": {
    "arbitrum": "Offchain Labs' own docs. The URL published in issue #59 (/build-decentralized-apps/reference/contract-addresses) 301s here.",
    "linea": "Consensys' own docs. The three URLs in issue #59 now 404; Linea restructured its docs and this is the live canonical page.",
    "optimism": "docs.optimism.io/superchain/addresses renders its L1 table CLIENT-SIDE from this file -- the URL is hardcoded in the page payload. It is Optimism's own first-party registry, and it is the only official source: no static docs.optimism.io page prints OP Mainnet's L1 addresses.",
    "across": "Across' own docs. The per-chain subpage URLs in issue #59 do not exist; the site serves one consolidated table.",
    "polygon": "Polygon's own static network registry, the file its SDKs and docs resolve mainnet addresses from. Five seeded rows: the three PoS PROXY addresses under POSContracts (RootChainManagerProxy, EtherPredicateProxy, ERC20PredicateProxy), plus the Plasma DepositManagerProxy (Main.Contracts.DepositManagerProxy) and the MRC20 native-token precompile (Matic.Contracts.Tokens.MaticToken, 0x0000000000000000000000000000000000001010). POL bridges via PLASMA, not the PoS RootChainManager: the deposit's L1 counterparty is the DepositManager, and its Polygon counterparty is the MRC20, which credits the bridged-in POL through the Bor state sync (#76). The proxies -- and the precompile the state sync writes from -- are what a user (or the system transaction) transacts with; the implementations behind them are never called directly and are not seeded.",
    "gnosis": "Gnosis Chain's own xDai Bridge page. It identifies the legacy Ethereum/Gnosis bridge proxies, the current Ethereum BridgeRouter and Gnosis USDS deposit entry points, and the Block Reward contract whose AddedReceiver log records consensus-minted xDAI credits.",
    "zksync": "Matter Labs' own ZKsync contract-address and JSON-RPC documentation. It identifies the current L1 ecosystem entry points, the Era chain contract, the legacy default ERC-20 bridge pair, and the L2 base-token system contract used for native withdrawals.",
    "zksync-lite": "Matter Labs' archived Lite environment documentation. It prints the mainnet rollup contract byte-for-byte; that same address is the L1 counterparty of the user's known 2021 deposit and 2022 withdrawal."
  },
  "excluded": {
    "hop": "Hop publishes no addresses in its documentation -- docs.hop.exchange defers to a TypeScript file on the SDK's `develop` branch, which is a moving target rather than a deployment record. Its address table also reuses one address for different contracts on different chains, and eth_address_labels is address-keyed with no chain column.",
    "stargate": "Same address-collision problem, inside Stargate's own tables (StargatePoolUSDC on Arbitrum and StargatePoolNative on OP Mainnet are both 0xe8cdf27a...), plus an unresolved V1/V2 split with two live doc sets. Both protocols remain one click away in the label form; seeding a per-token pool address globally would be a verdict about bytes verified on only one chain."
  },
  "explorerAccess": {
    "etherscan.io": "readable -- all three Polygon rows carry a \"Polygon (Matic): ...\" name tag that agrees with the first-party registry",
    "optimistic.etherscan.io": "readable",
    "lineascan.build": "readable",
    "arbiscan.io": "blocked -- HTTP 403 Cloudflare interstitial on every address, so no Arbitrum-side name tag was readable and none was substituted from elsewhere"
  },
